description:
Why: Clang's CIR codegen hoists ALL entry-block allocas (including anonymous compiler temps, not just real C locals) to the top of the function, and reuses the same debug 'name' attribute (e.g. "tmp") for structurally-independent temps -- most visibly the result-temp GNU statement-expressions (({ ... })) use to smuggle their trailing expression's value out of the block. Two SEPARATE, non-overlapping statement-expressions in the same function that resolve to different types (e.g. one long, one unsigned long) each get their own CIR alloca, both named "tmp" but with distinct SSA results (confirmed via emit-cir: %2 = alloca ... name="tmp" -> !s64i, %4 = alloca ... name="tmp" -> !u64i, both at entry-block level, both BEFORE either statement-expression's own cir.scope region).

src/lower.rs's lower_alloca derives the Rust identifier purely via sanitize_ident(attr_str(op, "name")...) with no check for whether that name is already bound to a DIFFERENT (still-live-for-later-code) SSA slot. Both allocas above get the literal identifier "tmp", so the lowerer emits:
  let mut tmp: i64 = 0;
  let mut tmp: u64 = 0;
both hoisted adjacently at the top of the function (matching CIR's entry-block order), BEFORE any of the interleaved executable code that references either one. Because Rust's let-shadowing takes effect immediately at the second declaration, ALL subsequent code -- including the FIRST statement-expression's own body, which is emitted textually AFTER both declarations -- ends up reading/writing the SECOND (u64) binding, even where it was meant to target the FIRST (i64) one. Result: 'tmp = x;' where x: i64 assigned into a now-u64 tmp -- a genuine E0308 type mismatch in the generated Rust, not just a style/naming issue.

Minimal repro (translate and note the produced Rust fails to typecheck -- 'tmp = x;' assigns an i64 into a u64 binding):
  int main() {
    int a = ({ long x = 5; x; });
    int b = ({ unsigned long y = 6; y; });
    return a + b;
  }
Confirmed present even with SLATE_RAW_LOWER=1 (i.e. this is a lowering bug, not a later fixup pass -- dead_locals/zero_init etc were investigated and ruled out; they only obscure the symptom further downstream by discarding what looks like a dead re-declaration).

Real-world instance: tests/fixtures.chibicc/unsupported/alignof/alignof.c has 15 same-named 'tmp' allocas across its many ASSERT(...) statement-expressions (5 !s64i, 10 !u64i in original source order) all collapsing the same way.

What: lower_alloca needs to detect a collision -- an incoming CIR alloca whose sanitized name already has an entry in self.slots pointing at a DIFFERENT, still-referenced SSA value -- and mint a disambiguated Rust name for the new one (e.g. a numeric suffix, mirroring how compiler temps elsewhere in this file are named uniquely). Care needed: must not disambiguate genuinely-nested, non-overlapping same-named locals that rely on ordinary Rust block-scoping today (e.g. repeated 'x' in separate { } scope blocks, which currently work correctly and must keep working) -- the distinguishing signal is that the colliding allocas are BOTH at the same (entry-block, hoisted) nesting level rather than in disjoint nested scopes.

Why: tests/fixtures/atomics.c translates correctly, but the generated Rust (tests/fixtures.generated/atomics.rs) is far from dense. Clang materializes per-call .atomictmp/atomic-temp allocas that Slate hoists as named locals, producing ~15 shadowed 'let mut _atomictmp'/'let mut atomic_temp' declarations at fn top, with the first store's value (100) fragilely fused into the LAST shadow's initializer by zero_init. Every atomic op re-derives 'unsafe { AtomicI32::from_ptr(std::ptr::addr_of_mut!(a)) }' even though 'a' is a non-escaping local. Results bounce through atomic_temp before reaching the named local. compare_exchange expands to a 4-temp chain (Result match + is_ok + double negation !_v36 + if). Unused stdatomic support items (memory_order enum, atomic_flag struct) are emitted regardless of use.

What to do:
- Fix operand/result temp forwarding for atomic ops in the baseline lowerer (src/lower.rs) so per-call CIR atomic allocas stop becoming hoisted shadowed locals.
- Then recover idiom in facts + fixups: promote non-escaping _Atomic locals to native AtomicN storage with safe method calls, inline single-use atomic result temps, densify the compare_exchange success/expected pattern, prune unused stdatomic support items.
- Extend fixture and fuzzer coverage (explicit orderings, cgen.rs + c.bnf).

Target shape for the fixture body:
    let a = AtomicI32::new(0);
    a.store(100, Ordering::SeqCst);
    let loaded = a.load(Ordering::SeqCst);
    let fa = a.fetch_add(5, Ordering::SeqCst);
    ...
    let ok = match a.compare_exchange(expected, 42, Ordering::SeqCst, Ordering::SeqCst) { Ok(_) => true, Err(v) => { expected = v; false } } as i32;

Rust-AST fixup that rewrites the synthesized `__slate_memchr` byte-scan loop into a slice + iterator form.

Baseline lowering emits `__slate_memchr` (src/lower.rs:~4313) as a hand-rolled index loop:

    fn __slate_memchr(s: *const c_void, c: i32, n: usize) -> *mut c_void {
        let b: u8 = c as u8;
        let bytes: *const u8 = s as *const u8;
        let mut i: usize = 0;
        while i < n {
            if unsafe { *bytes.add(i) } == b { return unsafe { bytes.add(i) } as *mut c_void; }
            i += 1;
        }
        return std::ptr::null_mut();
    }

Idiomatic target using .iter().position():

    fn __slate_memchr(s: *const c_void, c: i32, n: usize) -> *mut c_void {
        let b = c as u8;
        let bytes = s as *const u8;
        let haystack = unsafe { std::slice::from_raw_parts(bytes, n) };
        match haystack.iter().position(|&x| x == b) {
            Some(i) => unsafe { bytes.add(i) } as *mut c_void,
            None => std::ptr::null_mut(),
        }
    }

## Context
Follows the same idiomatization pattern as slate-04q.7/.4/.12. __slate_memchr is a synthesized runtime shim (not user code) built as a FnDef in src/lower.rs; the fixup could recognize the scan-loop-returning-pointer shape, or the helper's body could be emitted idiomatically at the source. n==0 must still return null; from_raw_parts requires bytes be valid for n even when n==0 (a dangling-but-aligned ptr is fine only if n==0 and libc callers pass valid s).
